SERVICES documentation, last updated on 23 Sep 2026

Network services -- NickServ, ChanServ and the like -- are a separate
program linked to the network as a server of its own (GNUWorld, X).
That is where they belong, and where this fork keeps them: the account
model in doc/readme.accounting is built on a services node sending
ACCOUNT, and nothing in this tree registers a nickname or keeps a
password.

What this file describes is the smaller thing that stayed: the pieces
in the core that let a module introduce a client the server speaks
through -- a bot -- and the user mode that marks one as a service of the
network.  A greeter, a help desk, a channel-side robot, the operator's
own /BOT: all of those, and not a services package.


THE PIECES
==========

  user mode +B     A bot the server introduced on its own behalf.  Only
                   a server sets it: a user can neither claim it nor
                   shed it, and neither can an operator.  Test it with
                   IsBot().

  user mode +S     A service bot of the network -- a bot that acts for
                   the network rather than for a person.  Set the same
                   way and by the same people as +B, and a MODE from
                   another server is trusted, as every user mode is.
                   Test it with IsServiceBot(); IsLocalServiceBot() is
                   true only for a service bot this server introduced,
                   which is the one whose messages this server handles.

                   What +S buys: the relay layer never delivers to it
                   (it has no connection); it may change another user's
                   modes through bot_set_user_mode(), within the limits
                   in doc/readme.accounting; and it is the one thing a
                   frozen user may talk to.

  include/bot.h    The bot API.  bot_create() introduces a client owned
                   by the calling module and destroyed when that module
                   unloads.  With BOT_SERVICE the client gets +S, +k, +o
                   and +B.  +k is what gives it the protections a channel
                   service has had since long before this file: no KICK,
                   no deop, no KILL from a local operator, no target
                   limits.  +o is the standing a service acts with: it is
                   counted and shown as an operator, and exempt from what
                   exempts one.  readme.modules, VIRTUAL CLIENTS,
                   explains the API.

  HOOK_MESSAGE_RECEIVED
                   Fires when a user -- on this server or any other --
                   sends a PRIVMSG or NOTICE to a local service bot, in
                   any of the forms the server accepts (a plain nick,
                   nick@server, a Pseudo{} alias), and, for each local
                   service bot on a channel, for every message to that
                   channel.  hc_client is the bot, hc_source the sender,
                   hc_channel the channel or NULL, hc_arg the text and
                   hc_notice whether it was a NOTICE.  The message goes
                   nowhere else: the bot has no connection.

  HOOK_CONFIG_LOADED
                   Fires once at start-up, when the configuration has
                   been read and the server is ready to introduce
                   clients, and at the end of every rehash.  A module
                   that reads configuration lists does it here, not in
                   mi_init: mi_init runs in the middle of the parse.

  modules/commands/m_bot/
                   /BOT, the operator's front end: CREATE, DESTROY,
                   RENAME, JOIN, PART, SAY, LIST.


A BOT IS A REAL CLIENT
======================

bot_create() calls make_client(&me, ...) with no connection, hashes it,
counts it and introduces it to the network in a NICK like any other
user.  It holds its nickname against everybody, everywhere, and it
joins channels, is in NAMES and is counted among the members.  It
quits with a QUIT when its module unloads.

That is the whole of it, and it is why a module that introduces a bot
has to think about the rest of the network.  Every server runs the same
modules -- the server enforces that and refuses a link where it is not
true (doc/readme.modules, ONE MODULE SET FOR THE WHOLE NETWORK) -- so a
module that creates a bot creates it on every server at once, and those
copies collide with each other, once per server, for ever.

There is no way around that in the core, and this fork stopped looking
for one.  A module that wants a bot per network rather than a bot per
server has to arrange it itself: introduce it only where a configured
name matches this server, or on the server the operator ran /BOT on.
The old irc_services module, which created a non-propagating local bot
on every server so that each one answered its own users, is gone --
along with its Service{} blocks, its published service configuration,
its nickname policy and the grace period it enforced.  What replaced it
is a services node, which is what every other ircu network has.


WHAT A SERVICE BOT MAY DO TO A USER
===================================

bot_set_user_mode(bot, target, "<modes>") -- a +S bot may change the
modes of any user who is not an operator, and is held to what a user
may set on itself: +o, +O, +k, +S, +B, +z and -x are dropped from the
string.  The rule is enforced on every server the change passes
through, so a peer that claims a bot did more is not believed.
doc/readme.accounting has the detail, including who may set +r and +f
and why a bot this server created is not among them.


PSEUDO{}
========

A Pseudo{} block gives users a short command for a service:

  Pseudo "NS" { name = "NickServ"; nick = "NickServ@services.example.net"; };

The target is a nick@server, which is how a user reaches a bot on the
services node without knowing where it is.  A Pseudo{} naming a bot this
server introduced works too, through the same nick@server path.


TESTS
=====

  tests/accounting/       a +S bot changing another user's modes, what
                          it may not change, and that it cannot touch
                          an operator
  tests/history/          a message to a +S bot is never stored
